epistle (0.12.3) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * The package requires podup 5.12.0 or newer, and `epistle stack` refuses an older one.
  * Expose per-account connection limits in the operator limits catalog.
  * Document how to expose the management API on a private interface, and warn
    when an API key pinned to a network sits behind a bridge that hides the
    address of the client.
  * WebDAV logs a failed sign-in with the client address behind a trusted reverse proxy and counts it against that address, as the API listeners do.
  * `epistle probe`, `status` and `stack update` check each listener on the host address it is really published on, so a management API pinned to a private address no longer counts as down.
  * Validation: one definition for each input rule that was copied (RFC 5321 atext, address and domain length limits, memory sizes and the invisible-formatting set), and a table of boundary cases that the entry paths must agree on.
  * migrate imap: the progress file no longer follows links, every command and the whole run have a deadline, and the memory kept per mailbox is bounded, so a hostile or slow source server cannot hold the process or its memory, and an interrupted copy resumes without losing or duplicating messages.
  * Backup: a database dump that stops part way is no longer kept as a backup, the SQL staged for a restore is written with owner-only permissions and removed once the restore finishes, and restored secrets and data directories are never readable by other users.
  * Backup and rollback: the leftovers of the reviews are closed, a restore killed by a signal no longer leaves its staged SQL behind for good, the host `psql` cannot block on a large dump, the SQL copied into the `db` container is forced to owner-only permissions before `psql` reads it, and the rollback store only saves version names and binaries it can later select, saying so when the upgrade skips a binary it already holds.
  * Security: webpki-roots and rustls-webpki are updated so certificate paths through the distrusted emSign roots are rejected (RUSTSEC-2026-0381, RUSTSEC-2026-0382 and RUSTSEC-2026-0383).
  * Security: stack rollback keeps its saved binaries in a store owned by root outside the directory of the service account, writes each copy through an exclusive temporary file, checks type, owner and digest before installing, ignores the store of earlier versions, and purge removes it.
  * `epistle init` asks its questions step by step with colour, an explanation and the default for each, shows a summary and asks for confirmation before it writes anything, and keeps what was answered when it is interrupted; with `--json`, without a terminal, with `NO_COLOR` or with `TERM=dumb` it behaves as before.
  * Packaging and release checks: an upgrade leaves the port floor the operator
    configured alone, purge stops the service before it removes anything, and
    the audit and release workflows verify and build the commit they publish.
  * migrate imap: a folder keeps a ledger of the messages it has confirmed, so a
    run resumed after an interruption does not bring back a message the user
    deleted or moved in the local mailbox, and an unreadable ledger stops the
    run with a message instead of importing anything again.
  * migrate imap: resuming a large folder does much less disk work, and the
    total deadline also stops the scan of the local mailbox between messages.
  * Clock: `epistle status` gains an external clock probe that compares
    three independent time sources, reports how many of them answered over
    NTS, and decides the verdict from the worst of them rather than from a
    median. The source list and the protocol per source are configurable, the
    probe is read-only, per-source and per-run deadlines bound the call, and
    `enabled = false` turns it off on a host with no route to the time sources.
  * An internationalised address local part no longer accepts the Unicode line and paragraph separators (U+2028, U+2029), as the Sieve script name and mailbox name rules already refused them.
  * An internationalised address local part no longer accepts the Unicode line and paragraph separators (U+2028, U+2029), as the Sieve script name and mailbox name rules already refused them. The static alias table and the structured addresses of a JMAP Email/set refuse them too.
  * `epistle init` compares the domain of the first account with the configured domains in the form the system stores, so an internationalised domain typed as a U-label matches its A-label.
  * `epistle init` refuses a first-account address already held by another account, alias, or masked address before it writes anything.
  * `epistle service set NAME --bind ADDRESS --port PORT` moves a listener that is already enabled, publishes the address on the host without touching the operator override, and `service list` shows where each service is published.
  * The guided setup test target no longer hangs when the whole suite runs, and
    no longer depends on how the run was started.
  * The guided setup tests create their terminals already private to themselves,
    take down every process they started when they give up, and fail when they
    could not check what they meant to check.
  * migrate imap: the documentation says what a resumed run really skips, what an
    unreadable ledger does, when only one migration of an account runs at a time
    and which messages a replay can only match against the mailbox.
  * A domain of one label, such as `localhost`, is refused wherever an address or a domain is created or configured, and the refusal says the domain needs at least one dot. A configuration file that held such an alias or forwarding target stops loading, so run `epistle config-check` before restarting after the upgrade.
  * Clock: the external probe tests cover the source deadline, the local
    clock step and the origin check against a local server, and the limit of
    the destination check with the NAT64 prefix of an operator is now written
    down in the documentation.
  * `epistle init`, `service` and `stack` read configuration, keys and the generated compose file through one reader that refuses a pipe, a device or a directory instead of waiting on it, the wait for the compose lock spends what the configuration lock left of the same 30 s, and `init` refuses a DNS `token_file` with a `$` before it writes anything. The documentation says that an `init` stopped part way is finished by running it again.
  * Upgrade note: `epistle service enable`, `service disable`, `init` and `stack update` publish WebDAV, the management API and autoconfig on `127.0.0.1` only, and a generated compose file that had one of them on every address of the host (`8090:8090`, `0.0.0.0:8090:8090` or `[::]:8090:8090`) is narrowed the next time one of those commands rewrites it. Each command says on stderr which mapping it narrowed. To reach one from another host, publish its port in `compose.override.yaml` behind a TLS reverse proxy.
  * Mailing lists keep their own identity on each delivered copy, a recipient addressed directly takes none, `epistle init` leaves a role address that an account or masked address holds alone, and DKIM covers `Disposition-Notification-To` and `Return-Receipt-To` when a message carries them.
  * Improve API authorization consistency.
  * Improve API authorization consistency: a key confined to some domains reads the delivery records of an account only while the sender is inside those domains, answers the same for another tenant's account and for one that does not exist on every route that takes an account name, and an account-bound key whose domains do not reach the account owns no push subscriptions. A secret can belong to one key only: adding a second key behind the same secret is refused, and a request whose secret matches two keys is served with the reach of neither.
  * API keys apply the same account and domain limits on every route.
  * Outgoing mail carries identification headers on every path: a Feedback-ID
    built from the domain and the mail class, List-Unsubscribe on bulk mail
    outside the marketing class, List-Id on list mail, Auto-Submitted on system
    mail, and optional Organization and X-Epistle values; each can be turned
    off, and the ones that identify or control replies are signed.
  * A submission with an ambiguous header name or line ending is refused before
    any header is changed, so the fields the server checks and the fields the
    signature covers are the ones a receiver sees.
  * A submission whose RFC 5322 header syntax, encoding or identity field
    counts differ between readers is refused, and outgoing line endings are
    normalised before signing and queueing so the signature covers the
    transmitted bytes.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0000

epistle (0.12.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Improve DNS provider inspection safety and accuracy.
  * Security: a banned account signs in with the right credentials from a network
    it has used before; from any other network the response stays the same and
    the ban cannot be probed for valid credentials.
  * Security: the known-network bypass lifts the account ban only, not the
    pair ban or operator-placed bans, and a successful sign-in via the
    bypass refreshes the bypass list without clearing the ban row.
  * Security: the SCRAM ban check at client-final re-reads the ban store
    instead of trusting the value computed at client-first, so a ban that
    fires or lapses between the two messages is enforced or lifted by the
    server that actually completes the proof.
  * Security: the hourly purge of the known-network rows now respects the
    window the operator set, so a row past the configured horizon is dropped
    and one inside it stays.
  * Queue: record API cancellations in the delivery log and notify webhooks.
  * Security: keep DNS provider tokens in private secret files and warn about
    legacy credential sources.
  * Security: improve API client attribution and retry guidance.
  * Security: tighten the `trusted_proxies` allowlist (reject `/0`, deduplicate, cap).
  * DKIM: sign more message headers and protect important fields from additions.
  * DKIM: long signature fields fold within the line limit and unsafe field names
    are not signed; dual-signed recipients carry one ed25519 and one RSA signature.
  * Add advisory DNS provider credential checks.
  * `epistle status` and `init` no longer wait for a host clock probe that does
    not answer, and a line of the message body no longer changes which
    `Resent-*` headers a DKIM signature covers.
  * Security: sanitize trust headers on inbound mail, closing the gaps
    left when mail-parser 0.11.9 and the local DKIM/ARC readers disagree
    about how a reserved header name folds.
  * Security: IMAP ID no longer discloses the server version.
  * Status reports API key configuration warnings.
  * Security: restore the /api/v1/auth/verify reach the panel had before the
    tenant-boundary change, so a server-supplied account the mail listeners
    accept cannot be silently dropped from the panel sign-in path; a
    domain-confined key still fails closed on every account the static +
    dynamic listing cannot resolve, and the limit is documented.
  * Validate listener connection caps and warn when the host open-file limit
    leaves little room for the generated mail service.
  * Security: cap the TLS connections one client address may hold open at once
    on every listener that terminates TLS in the server (the autoconfig
    listener over HTTPS, MTA-STS). A connection over the cap is closed before
    the handshake, the same `503`/`BYE`/`-ERR` is answered over plain text as
    the peer sees, and the place is released however the connection ends.
  * Security: ManageSieve now holds an unauthenticated connection to one
    deadline for the whole phase, counted from the start of the session and
    covering every read, including the data of a command. Bytes arriving
    slowly no longer extend it.
  * Security: IMAP and POP3 hold an unauthenticated connection to the same
    kind of deadline: one for the whole phase before sign-in, counted from the
    start of the session, covering the TLS handshake, every command and
    every literal, which neither the bytes that arrive nor a stream of cheap
    commands extend. Before, each read had its own timeout of 30 minutes
    (IMAP) or 10 minutes (POP3), so one byte just before it ran out kept a
    connection open for as long as the client liked. A signed-in session is
    not held to it. The deadline is 60 seconds by default and is set for the
    three protocols with `preauth_timeout` or `epistle limits set
    preauth-timeout`.
  * Security: the same pre-authentication deadline also bounds every byte the
    server tries to write or flush before sign-in (greeting, responses, error
    BYE/-ERR, post-STARTTLS re-greeting) on IMAP, POP3 and ManageSieve. A
    peer that stops reading is cut off at the deadline, releasing the
    admission slot; before, a blocked output kept the connection alive past
    the bound.
  * Security: close the gaps the previous deadline left in the IMAP helpers
    that drain a rejected literal and a chained literal announced after it,
    and in the ManageSieve UNAUTHENTICATE response that left the session
    signed out under the expired pre-auth clock of the original sign-in.
  * Security: one client address can no longer hold every connection of a
    listener. IMAP, POP3 and ManageSieve cap the connections one client holds
    (200 by default, 40 for ManageSieve, `max_mailbox_connections_per_ip`), and the plain-HTTP
    listeners cap both their connections (1024, `max_http_connections`) and
    what one client holds (256, `max_http_connections_per_ip`); an IPv6 client
    is counted by its /64, as for SMTP. A connection over a cap gets the
    protocol's `BYE`, `-ERR` or `503` and is closed before any handshake, and
    its place is released however the connection ends. All three are set with
    `epistle limits`. Behind a proxy or NAT that hides the client address every
    client shares one count, so raise them there.
  * The generated `mail` service raises its open-file limit to 65536 (and the
    sample systemd unit sets `LimitNOFILE=65536`). A rootless container
    started with the default soft limit of 1024 stopped greeting on ports 25
    and 587 at around a thousand idle connections. The request is lowered to
    the hard limit of the host when that is smaller, because Podman refuses to
    start a container above it. Run `epistle init` again to regenerate an
    existing stack's compose file.
  * Limits follow the standards: every adjustable limit has an industry-standard
    default and the operator may change it; rules the standards make mandatory
    (MUST) cannot be crossed, and rules they only recommend (SHOULD) print a
    warning. `epistle limits show` and `--json` carry the kind (`adjustable`,
    `recommended`, `fixed`) and the RFC reference for every limit, and the
    same table backs `docs/limits.md` so the operator-facing page stays in
    step with the code.
  * Security: tighten the SPF §4.6.4 budget enforcement, the catalog's
    metadata for the limits the standards only recommend, and the
    rejection of misconfigured connection-cap keys; the operator-facing
    table in `docs/limits.md` now mirrors the in-process catalog exactly.
  * An autoconfig listener with TLS and no `max_connections_per_listener` set
    refused every connection, because the unset cap reached it as 0. It now
    uses the default of 1024.
  * Security: the Bayesian filter no longer lets one account decide what
    another receives. A message with more than one local recipient, or
    addressed to an alias or a list, used to be scored by the corpus of the
    first local account of the envelope (the first member of an alias), and
    that verdict (Junk, or a `550` at DATA) applied to every recipient, so an
    account that taught its own corpus a spammer's text as ham could let that
    spam through to anyone addressed alongside it. An account's own corpus now
    scores a message only when that account is the single local recipient,
    was addressed as itself and has at least 200 ham and 200 spam of its own.
    Everything else is scored by the shared corpus when that has as much,
    which changes delivery: mail to aliases, lists and accounts that have not
    trained their own corpus is now subject to `bayes_junk_at` and
    `bayes_reject_at` through the shared corpus, where before it was scored
    only when the first member had a trained corpus of its own.
  * Security: training the Bayesian corpus on a message is bounded. The shared
    corpus learns from every message the server accepts or rejects, whoever
    sent it, and it tokenized the whole message (up to 25 MiB, a few million
    words) and wrote one row per word, one statement each, holding a lock
    that the lessons of the accounts waited for. A message now teaches the
    corpus from at most 5000 distinct words of the first and last 128 KiB of
    what it says, in one statement; when it has more, the first and last 1000
    are kept and the rest chosen by a keyed hash of the word, so where the
    sender puts the others decides nothing. The transactions of a training and
    of `epistle account remove` now take their locks in one order, which ends
    the deadlocks between them. The shared corpus is not pruned: a message of
    5000 new words adds about 1.7 MB of table and 3.5 MB of log, and one
    address may teach 30 such messages an hour.
  * Security: the Bayesian filter follows a deeper message structure, up to 32
    levels and 10 000 parts where it was 10 and 1000. The cost of reading
    stays linear in the size of the message, and a message past either limit
    is still read as raw bytes and still counts as not read in full.
  * Security: scoring a message with the Bayesian corpus is bounded the same
    way. It tokenized, hashed and looked up every word of a message of up to
    25 MiB inside the session of an unauthenticated sender (338 MiB of heap
    for one message). The filter now reads what a person reads: the headers
    and the text parts, decoded, not attachments or images, and whitespace and
    one-letter words cost nothing; a message that two readers could split
    into parts differently is read as raw bytes as well. A message under 128 KiB + 128 KiB of
    words and 5000 distinct words scores on all of it, as before; one with an
    attachment now scores as the message without it (before, any attachment of
    more than about 125 KiB gave the message a score of 0.5). A message over
    either bound is scored on part of it and never below 0.5, which is not
    Junk with the default `bayes_junk_at` of 0.95 and only reaches a second
    opinion with `[antispam_llm]`: spam padded with more than 256 KiB of words,
    or with more than 5000 distinct words, with the spam in the middle, can
    escape the filter. That is the detection lost to the bound.
  * Security: the shared Bayesian corpus no longer starts a task per message,
    and a sender cannot decide it alone. The server queues what it learns
    (64 jobs, 2 workers) instead of spawning a task for each message. Ham and
    spam are budgeted apart: a client address and the wider block around it
    (IPv4 /32 and /24, IPv6 /64 and /48) may add 30 and 120 jobs per hour,
    a sender domain 30 but only when the server verified it (the envelope
    sender is written by the client, so a forged domain spends nothing of the
    real one), a whole network (IPv4 /16, IPv6 /32) 125 and 16 in flight,
    and the whole server 2000, the last quarter of them kept for networks that
    have used few. The tables of keys drop the least recently used entry when
    full instead of refusing new senders. A job over its budget or that finds
    the queue full is dropped, without spending any budget when it was the
    queue that had no room, and counted in `mail_bayes_training_dropped_total`.
    The server also learns ham only from a sender whose SPF or DKIM passed,
    so an unverified sender cannot teach the corpus that its text is ham;
    bounces, SubjectPass resends and mail from a sender with neither teach
    nothing. A sender that fails the checks can still have text of its
    choosing learned as spam within its budget. Behind a container network
    that hides the client address (rootless Podman with a bridge) the server
    sees one address and learns at the rate of one.
  * Security: the Bayesian filter reads mail the way a client shows it, in
    more cases. Attachments no longer use up the budget for decoding text, so
    text sent as base64 or quoted-printable after a large file is read; a part
    that declares two transfer encodings is read under each; text in UTF-16 or
    an 8-bit charset, and RFC 2047 encoded words in the headers, are read as a
    person sees them; zero width characters and soft hyphens no longer split a
    word; and a message the filter could not follow (too deep, too many parts,
    a text part or a message inside it past the 2 MiB decoding budget) counts
    as not read in full, so it scores at least 0.5. Letters outside ASCII are
    now lowercased as well: a token with a capital outside ASCII ("Привет",
    "École") that the corpus learned before this release is not found under
    its new lower case form and is learned again; the old rows stay in the
    table, unused, because the corpus keeps keyed hashes and cannot convert
    them. Tokens in ASCII are unchanged. A message that cannot be reduced at all
    teaches nothing and spends none of the sender's budget.
  * JMAP: Sieve scripts (RFC 9661). The session advertises
    `urn:ietf:params:jmap:sieve`, and `SieveScript/get`, `/query`, `/set` and
    `/validate` manage the same scripts ManageSieve does: a script written
    over either is listed, valid and active in the other, with an id that
    survives a rename. Exactly one script is active, switched with
    `onSuccessActivateScript` and `onSuccessDeactivateScript`. The limits
    apply to both protocols: 1 MiB per script, 50 scripts, names of 128
    characters and 200 octets, and a `require` of an extension the
    interpreter does not run is refused (ManageSieve answers
    `NO (QUOTA/MAXSIZE)` and `NO (QUOTA/MAXSCRIPTS)`, and HAVESPACE checks
    them). A refusal names the line and never repeats the script. See
    docs/configuration.md.
  * Sieve: a script is now bounded in what one run may spend. `:matches` no
    longer overflows the stack or takes exponential time on a pattern of many
    wildcards (a 100 KB script aborted the server), and a text of any length
    still matches; `set "a" "${a}${a}"` repeated no longer builds gigabytes
    (variables are cut to 4096 octets, 64 KiB per run); a run is stopped past
    20000 steps or 256 MiB of work (what it decided about where the
    message goes stands, what it would have sent is dropped); `fileinto` files
    into a folder once and into at most 8 folders; no chain of `not` nests past
    64 levels (about 200000 of them overflowed the stack). A filter that files
    by a header (`fileinto "${1}"`) makes no folder past 200 in the account,
    counting every level, and keeps the message in INBOX instead.
  * Sieve `redirect`: at most 4 addresses per message and an address once, and
    the target must be a valid address (a line break in it was written into
    the outbound `RCPT TO` command). A redirect to something that is not an
    address keeps the message instead of dropping it behind a redirect that
    never happened. Upgrading: an active script that redirects to more than 4
    different addresses now forwards to the first 4 and the log says how many
    it ignored; a script that relied on `redirect` of a malformed address now
    leaves the message in INBOX.
  * Sieve scripts (ManageSieve and JMAP, one check): a `require` of an extension
    the interpreter does not run is refused when saving, and the list now
    includes `subaddress`, `comparator-i;octet` and `comparator-i;ascii-casemap`.
    Upgrading: a stored script that requires an unsupported extension keeps
    running but cannot be saved again until it is fixed; names with U+2028 or
    U+2029, with white space at either end, with a bidirectional control or not
    in normal form C are no longer listed (the log names the account once;
    rename the files in `<data_dir>/accounts/<account>/sieve/`); an account
    with more than 50 scripts keeps them all and cannot add one until it is
    under the limit. The active marker is no longer trimmed.
  * Sieve: what a test reads of a message is a fixed window: the first 1 MiB
    of the body, and per header name of a lookup the first 1000 values and
    64 KiB (16 names at most), so no header hides the others listed with it.
    Only the first 10 000 headers of a message are read, and mail with more
    than 9 900 header fields is refused before a filter reads it (554 5.6.0 at
    DATA and BDAT, 422 on /api/v1/send, forbiddenToSend on JMAP
    EmailSubmission/set, counted in rejected_header_fields) so that none can
    hide behind padding. Upgrading: a header value is read with the blanks at
    its ends dropped (space, tab, VT, FF, CR, NEL, NBSP and the other Unicode
    white space) and every run of blanks inside it as one space, so a key with
    two spaces or a tab inside no longer matches; an octet that is not UTF-8
    is a "?". The interpreter keeps no more than that, 64 KiB of each header
    and the first 1 MiB of the body in memory: a
    message of two million headers took 620 MiB per recipient with a filter.
    A `:contains` test with eight or more keys reads the text once, not once
    per key: a block-list of a thousand words over a mebibyte of body used to
    make the run give up and skip the rules after it.
    Upgrading: a stored script with a `:matches` key over 256 characters or 32
    wildcards no longer matches (the test is false, the rest of the script
    runs but from that test on it keeps the message instead of discarding or
    rejecting it, so a `not` around such a key cannot lose mail, and the log
    names the account once an hour); it used to match. A
    message bigger than that no longer makes a script run out of work, and a rule that matches text past the
    window does not see it: the log says so once an hour per account and the
    new counter `mail_sieve_inspection_truncated_total` (alert metric
    `sieve_inspection_truncated`) counts the messages. A run that does run
    out keeps its `discard`, `fileinto` and flags, drops its `redirect`,
    `reject` and `vacation`, and keeps the message if anything outward was
    dropped. A `set` that does not fit now stops the run instead of being
    ignored, and the numbered match variables no longer share the 64 KiB of
    the script's own variables. See docs/configuration.md.
  * JMAP: `VacationResponse/set` that switches the reply on, or changes one
    that is on, takes the `send` scope as well as `write`, like a Sieve script
    that replies; a key with `read` and `write` only gets `forbidden`.
    Switching it off needs only `write`.
  * JMAP: storing or activating a Sieve script that redirects, replies or
    rejects takes the `send` scope as well as `write`. `SieveScript/set` is one
    step under the account lock (`ifInState` is exact, the active script changes
    without a moment of no filter), and waiting for that lock no longer holds a
    runtime worker.
  * Security: every way of signing in now applies the same account rules.
    Until now SCRAM-SHA-256 (SMTP AUTH and IMAP AUTHENTICATE, only when
    `[auth] scram` is on) skipped the account's second factor and its
    `disabled` flag, and an OAUTHBEARER/XOAUTH2 token or a TLS client
    certificate (SASL EXTERNAL) skipped `disabled`, so a disabled account
    could still sign in, read its mail and send. That was present in 0.12.0 and
    0.12.1. A disabled account is refused by every mechanism, an account with a
    TOTP secret is refused by SCRAM (the proof is derived from the password
    alone and cannot carry the code; it signs in with PLAIN or LOGIN and the
    code appended to the password, as before), and the per-account protocol
    list applies to all of them. A refused SCRAM exchange ends as an ordinary
    wrong password. If you rely on SCRAM for an account with two-factor
    authentication, that account now uses PLAIN or LOGIN.
  * Security: SCIM now honours the domains of the API key. A key created with
    `--domain` and the `scim` scope used to list, read, change the password of,
    deactivate, create and delete accounts of every tenant. It now reaches only
    the accounts that sit entirely inside its domains (the others answer 404),
    and a user created or replaced with an address outside them is refused
    with 400. `POST /scim/v2/Users` also stops at the tenant's `max_accounts`
    with 409, which SCIM used to skip, and so does a `PUT` that moves an
    account into a tenant at its cap. A `userName` already held by another
    tenant answers like an unusable one. The `startIndex` query parameter
    is honoured now (only `start_index` was). This was present in 0.12.0 and 0.12.1:
    if you issued `scim` keys with `--domain`, rotate them.
  * SCIM user lists now report the total number of matching resources independently
    of the current page.

  * Security: an API key confined to some domains (`--domain`) now stays inside
    them on every route, not only on the accounts routes. It could list and
    remove the queue entries of every tenant, read and clear the shared
    suppression list, mint, change and remove masked addresses and read and
    restore the archive of another tenant's account, read server-wide counters,
    and use `POST /api/v1/auth/verify` to check whether a password is right for
    any account. Such a key now sees and touches only its own tenant (`404` for
    anything else), the shared suppression list answers `403`, `status` leaves
    out `queue_size`, and `auth/verify` answers `valid: false` for accounts
    outside the key's domains without trying the password. Creating an account
    whose name another tenant holds answers like an unusable name. This was
    present in 0.12.0 and 0.12.1: if you issued keys with `--domain`, rotate
    them. A test now lists every route with its domain rule and fails when a
    route is added without one.
  * Security: an unknown login can no longer be told from a real one. SCRAM
    refused an unknown user at the first message and challenged a real one, and
    a failed PLAIN, LOGIN, POP3 or ManageSieve check for an unknown, disabled or
    receive-only login returned in microseconds while a real account took a
    full password hash. SCRAM now answers every login with a challenge (a
    refused or unknown login gets decoy credentials with a salt that is stable
    per login and that a client cannot recompute) and ends at the proof, and
    the password check does the same hashing work in every case. A refused
    SCRAM attempt counts as a failed attempt when the exchange ends instead of
    at the first message.
  * Security: the shared authentication ban counts failures harder to dodge.
    A correct login used to clear the failure count of the client address, so
    someone with an account of their own could log in between guesses at
    others and never reach the ban; a success now clears only the account and
    the account-from-this-client pair. An IPv6 client is counted as its /64
    (rotating addresses inside it no longer resets the count). And five
    failures from anywhere used to lock the victim's account for its owner:
    failures are now counted per account and client (`pair:` in `ban list`),
    and the account itself is locked at 20 failures in 15 minutes, which one
    client cannot reach but a guess spread over many clients does. An
    allow-listed client is never banned on its address or /64 (a neighbour's
    ban of the /64 does not apply to it), but its failures still count on the
    account (not on the pair, so typos behind a shared proxy do not lock one
    account's owner out of it), so a trusted network cannot guess without limit. A
    route with no client address counts as one client `unknown`, and WebDAV
    now passes the client address. A NAT or proxy address shares one count
    among everyone behind it; allow-list it if that hurts.
  * Security: the REST API counts wrong bearer tokens per client (an IPv4
    address or an IPv6 /64) instead of one count for everyone, so one client
    cannot lock the rest out with wrong tokens, and a valid token from another
    client does not give it back its attempts. An IPv6 /48 has eight times a
    client's budget and there is a ceiling of 2000 failures from anyone in a
    window; once either is spent, a request with a wrong token answers `429`
    instead of `401`. Neither is ever asked of a request with a valid token,
    so other people's failures cannot keep it out. A client's own budget still
    applies before its token is looked at, and the tables are bounded.
  * Security: wrong bearer tokens are cheaper to refuse and a right one no
    longer gives a client its failures back. A call with a valid token used to
    clear that client's count, so someone holding one key could guess, call
    and guess again; the count now only ages out with the window. A token
    that is empty or over 1024 bytes is refused before any hashing. With a
    legacy argon2id `token_hash`, every wrong token cost an argon2 on the
    request's own thread. API keys are now looked up first (SHA-256) and never
    touch the configured hash; the argon2 verification runs off the async
    workers, two at a time at most (up to 64 more wait their turn; past that a
    request is told `429`), and a request's failure is counted before it
    waits, so requests arriving together cannot all pass a client's limit. Once the real token has been seen a wrong bearer is told
    apart by a SHA-256 digest and costs no argon2. Guesses from many clients
    are still not capped as a whole: the protection is the entropy of the
    token, so use a long random one.
  * Security: a refused password check now costs the same whatever the login
    holds. An account with app passwords paid one hash per app password on top
    of the primary one (3.2 times an unknown login with two of them), which
    told an attacker which accounts exist and which have app passwords. Every
    refusal now runs the hashes of the account with the most, real ones and
    stand-ins alike.
  * Security: the ban store is asked the same questions and written the same
    way for a login that does not exist as for a real one (it used to make one
    call where a real account made three, which with PostgreSQL behind it and
    SCRAM, where no password hash hides it, took a third as long), so the time
    of a refusal no longer says which logins exist. Each unknown login is
    counted under a stand-in of its own (a keyed hash of the login), so it
    reaches the same bans as a real account and a ban on one is not visible
    from another.
  * Security: a device grant that has been approved can no longer be approved
    again. A second account that knew the `user_code` replaced the first
    approval, and the device that was polling received the second account's
    token. This was present in 0.12.0 and 0.12.1.
  * Security: `POST /oauth/device_authorization`, which needs no credentials,
    kept every grant it created in memory until someone polled for it, so an
    unauthenticated caller could grow the server's memory without limit. A
    client (an IPv4 address or an IPv6 /64) may now hold 10 pending grants,
    an IPv6 /48 100, and the server 10000. At a cap the expired grants are
    dropped first; a client or /48 still over its cap gets `429 slow_down`,
    and when only the server's total is full the oldest pending grant of the
    group holding the most is evicted, but only if that group holds more than
    the requester does (an approved grant never is, and with no group above
    its share the call is refused), so a flood from a few prefixes cannot keep
    everyone else from starting a flow, nor push out the grants of light ones.
  * Security: the webhook no longer follows redirects. A `307` from the
    endpoint used to resend the event, with its `X-Webhook-Signature`, to
    wherever it pointed, an internal address included. A redirect now counts
    as a failed delivery, and `epistle webhook test` reports its status.
  * CLI: the help of `epistle attachments check --strict` said it exits with 1
    when the verdict is quarantine or reject; it exits with 3, as
    docs/cli.md says.
  * CLI: `epistle evidence export` run as root printed the staging directory
    of the delegated run (text and the `bundle` field of `--json`); it now
    prints the path given to `--out`.
  * Evidence: `epistle evidence timestamp disable` run while the first day the
    log ever had was closed and not yet sealed made the server seal that day
    as untimestamped, which the next check refused as a downgrade and held
    `transactional` mail until it was acknowledged. A day is now sealed in the
    mode the log has for it, whatever the settings file says at that moment;
    `epistle evidence key rotate`, which seals the closed days too, had the
    same defect and no longer reads the settings file either.
  * Status: the `resources` warning that the containers may use more than 85%
    of the host's memory named one `epistle stack resources set` command, and
    on an install that kept the limits of an older release (7 GiB of limits on
    a 5.8 GiB host) running it left the warning in place. It now lists the
    commands that bring the sum under the line together, and says the host
    needs more memory when the floors of the services do not get there.
  * The outbound suppression markers and their directories are created with
    explicit modes (0600 and 0700), like the rest of the spool, and no longer
    with whatever umask the process that wrote the first one had. Markers
    and directories written by earlier releases keep the mode they have.
  * Docs and tests: the rule for who may send as which address is written
    down and pinned on SMTP submission, the API and JMAP (dotted and
    upper-case addresses, lists, a list in another served domain; a sub-address,
    the catch-all and a domain alias are refused), and the difference between
    the account-name rule and the address rule is tabled per command in
    `docs/cli.md`. No behaviour changes.
  * `epistle status` has a new `clock` area that reports whether the host
    keeps its clock synchronised with a time source, read from the kernel
    (`adjtimex`, no root needed) with `timedatectl` as the fallback. A clock
    that is not synchronised, or that cannot be read, is a warning with the
    commands that fix it; nothing is changed on the host. `epistle init`
    prints the same warning and carries on. The package now recommends
    `systemd-timesyncd | chrony`, and no longer suggests `postgresql`, which
    runs in a container of the stack.
  * Security: message data sent with BDAT is held to the same line framing as
    DATA. A bare CR, a bare LF, a NUL or a line over 998 octets now ends the
    connection with `554` (`500` for the length), whichever way the data is cut
    into chunks, and the same message gets the same verdict over either
    command. A final chunk that ends in a CR is refused too, and so is a last
    line left without a CRLF that the sender would put over the line limit.
    A line that begins with a dot is held to the same length as DATA holds
    it. A message whose last line has no CRLF is otherwise still accepted and
    stored as sent.
  * Antispam: an account's corpus learns from where its user puts mail. A
    message copied or moved into Junk (`Junk`, `Spam` or `Rejects`) trains
    spam; a message moved out of Junk to any mailbox but Trash trains ham.
    IMAP COPY and MOVE and the mailbox change of JMAP `Email/set` take the
    decision; `$Junk` and `$NotJunk` keep working and agree with it. Each
    message counts once whatever route it took (the server keeps a keyed hash
    of what each account learned, in the new `bayes_learned` table), and a
    message the user moves back takes the earlier lesson back before the new
    one is added. What an account learned before this release is in the
    corpus but not in that record: it counts again if the user moves it and
    cannot be taken back (`learning status` shows it as `unrecorded`).
    Learning is bounded. A command queues at most 100 lessons, an account has
    32 places of the 256 in the queue, 200 lessons a minute and 5000 a day,
    and may keep the one worker busy for 10 seconds a minute, counted from the
    work of its own lessons, failed ones included, and not from the time they
    waited. A message is
    read as the SMTP path reads it (headers and text parts, no attachments, in
    the charset it declares), and a lesson touches at most 5000 tokens of it,
    the cap the server's own training uses; a message that cannot be read
    teaches nothing and spends none of the lessons or tokens of the account,
    though the time spent trying to read it is charged to it. An account holds at most 250000 token rows
    and spends at most 1000000 tokens a day, and its record keeps 100000
    messages, forgetting the oldest tenth when full. Lessons over a limit are
    dropped and counted in `mail_bayes_training_dropped_total`, and the ones
    lost for another reason in `mail_bayes_learning_unreadable_total`,
    `mail_bayes_learning_failed_total` and `mail_bayes_learning_refused_total`.
    The record and the counts change in one transaction, so a lesson that
    cannot be recorded is not trained. Migration 0008 adds two tables and no
    destructive statement.
  * CLI: `epistle learning status|enable|disable|reset`. `status` prints what
    each account's corpus learned (spam, ham, taken back, repeated, refused,
    throttled, messages recorded, token rows, unrecorded, corpus size);
    `disable` and `enable` switch learning per account without a restart;
    `reset` drops an account's corpus and its record and keeps the switch, so
    an account switched off stays off, and needs `--yes` off a terminal. It
    needs `[database]` and exits 3 without it.
  * API: `GET /api/v1/openapi.json` serves an OpenAPI 3.1 description of
    every HTTP route (the `read` scope, like `status`), and `docs/api.md` and
    `docs/headers.md` describe the API and the headers of the mail it sends.
    Tests fail when a route, a scope, a header name, an error code name or a
    number quoted in the guide changes and the description does not.
  * Security: signing in with a password (SMTP, IMAP, POP3, ManageSieve,
    WebDAV and the API) takes the same time and does the same work whatever
    the login is. A right password, or a right app password, for an account
    that may not sign in through that protocol was refused sooner than a
    wrong one; it now costs the same. The password checks also run a few at a
    time and off the threads that serve the connections, so a burst of
    sign-in attempts no longer holds up the other listeners or grows the
    memory with the number of connections. While the line of checks is full,
    a sign-in is answered with the temporary failure of its protocol (IMAP
    `NO [UNAVAILABLE]`, SMTP `454 4.7.0`, POP3 `-ERR [SYS/TEMP]`) and not as
    a wrong password; it counts against nobody and shows in
    `mail_auth_hash_gate_rejected_total`.
  * `epistle stack update`, which the package runs when it upgrades, waits for
    Podman's API to answer before it changes anything, and runs a podup step
    again, up to four times, when podup reports that its connection to Podman
    dropped (`connection closed before message completed`), waiting for the
    API between runs. Before, one such drop during the upgrade ended with the
    warning that the stack did not come back healthy, and the operator had to
    run `epistle stack update` by hand. Any other podup failure is still
    reported at the first attempt.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Tue, 20 Oct 2026 05:39:47 +0000

epistle (0.12.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Security: Namecheap sent its API key in the query of the `getHosts` GET,
    and a failed connection or a timeout printed the whole URL, key
    included, in the error of `dns lockdown`, `dns provider test` and the
    DKIM daemon. `getHosts` is now a form POST like `setHosts`, and no DNS
    provider puts the request URL in an error.
  * Security: the payload of an IMAP APPEND refused over its size limit, and
    the bytes of an SMTP BDAT chunk refused for memory or sent outside a
    transaction, were run as commands. They are now read and discarded.
  * Security: rustls 0.23.46 fixes RUSTSEC-2026-0346 (secrets in the Debug
    output of client session values), 0347 (application data sent to the outer
    server after an ECH rejection), 0348 (Brotli certificate decompression that
    could allocate 1 GiB before authentication) and 0349 (a ServerHello that
    could downgrade later connections to classical key exchange). serde_json
    1.0.154 and opentelemetry-otlp 0.33.1 are updated too.
  * Security: with `encrypt_at_rest`, the outbound spool envelopes (sender,
    recipients, account, retry state) are sealed with the storage key and bound
    to their id. Envelopes written by 0.12.0 are still delivered and are sealed
    the next time they are read or edited. Spool files are created 0600 and
    directories 0700 whatever the umask, and the server audits and tightens
    them at start-up through no-follow descriptors; if it cannot, it refuses to
    start. `epistle queue`, `status` and `account remove --queue discard` open
    the spool with the configured key, and `status` reports spool encryption
    and loose permissions.
  * Security: inbound SMTP memory is bounded. `[server] message_memory_budget`
    is shared by every DATA and BDAT transaction, a client (an IPv4 address or
    an IPv6 /64) may hold at most a quarter of it, and a refused transaction
    gets `452 4.3.1`. Unset, it is a fifth of the memory of the `mail`
    container (its cgroup limit, else `mem_limit` in compose.yaml, else 1 GiB
    assumed), between `max_message_size` and 1 GiB; a value set by hand is
    kept, with a warning when it would take over 70% of the container.
    `max_connections_per_ip` (default 20, counted per IPv6 /64) is new: with
    rootless bridge networking every client arrives as one address, so all of
    them share the 20. `max_message_size` now also reaches the REST send route,
    which never received it in production.
  * Security: a client that sends a message too slowly no longer holds its
    memory. After `data_rate_grace` (120 s) the average rate of a DATA or BDAT
    transaction must stay at `min_data_rate` (10240 B/s) or the client gets
    `421 4.4.2 transfer too slow`. A session gets `421 4.7.0` after
    `junk_command_limit` (100) commands without progress (NOOP, RSET, unknown
    commands, refused MAIL or RCPT, and so on) and `421 4.4.2` after
    `max_session_time` (1800 s), never in the middle of a message body. `0`
    turns each off. All of them are set with `epistle limits set|unset|show`,
    as is `message_memory_budget`.
  * Fix: the per-account and per-domain submission rate limits never reached
    the server. One limiter per account now covers SMTP, REST and JMAP, each
    accepted recipient costs one unit, a charge is given back when the message
    is refused later or the send fails, and the refusal reads `sending rate
    limit exceeded; retry later` everywhere. An account in several limited
    domains gets the strictest limit. `epistle limits set submission-rate 0` is
    refused; use `unset`. A JMAP send without an envelope goes to the
    deduplicated To, Cc and Bcc only (RFC 8621), no longer to Resent-*
    addresses of an earlier copy.
  * Fix: inbound `rsa-sha256` DKIM signatures never verified. The verifier gave
    the crypto library the whole key record, a SubjectPublicKeyInfo as senders
    publish it, where it expects the bare RSA key. Mail from senders that sign
    only with RSA had no passing DKIM, so DMARC could align only through SPF.
    The verifier now unwraps the record and refuses malformed DER.
  * Upgrading from 0.12.0 needs no SQL migration. Without changing any setting,
    `epistle status` now fails on a mail name (MX target, SMTP host,
    autoconfig, autodiscover, mta-sts) behind the Cloudflare proxy, on a DNSSEC
    TLSA record that does not match the certificate in use, and on a duplicate
    SPF, DMARC, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT or DKIM record, and it warns when the
    container limits add up to over 85% of the host's memory. The default of
    `clamd_max_bytes` is 26279936 (it was 26214400). A configuration without
    `clamd_on_error` keeps `accept`, without `[attachments]` keeps `flag`, and
    the warm-up stays off; on a new install `epistle init` writes `defer` and
    `quarantine` and offers the warm-up. The `enable` and `disable` actions of
    `domain`, `alert`, `webhook` and `relay`, and `attachments policy
    set|unset`, write mail.toml and restart the `mail` container when the stack
    is installed, and otherwise say that the server must be restarted.
  * CLI: every command that prints data takes `--json` and then prints one
    JSON document on stdout, with notes and errors on stderr; the hidden flat
    forms (`accounts`, `account-add`, `app-password-create`, `api-keys` and
    the rest) take it too. The commands whose stdout is already the product
    (`serve`, `local`, `mta-sts-serve`, `export`, `backup`, `completions`,
    `report-abuse`, `mobileconfig`, `autoconfig`, `autodiscover`) and
    `stack logs` do not. docs/cli.md lists the result object.
  * CLI exit code 3 is new: the command is well formed and its target
    exists, but a rule of the server refuses the change. It replaces exit 2
    in four cases, so a script can tell "refused by policy or state" from "fix
    the command" (2) and "retry later" (1): a static account that only the
    configuration file may change (`account disable|remove|password|...`,
    `app-password create`), a limit that is reached (the masked-address
    limit, the other store limits), the `scim` scope on an API key bound to an
    account, and `account forward add` while `srs_secret` is not set.
    Unknown names, rejected input and a destructive action without `--yes`
    stay 2. A script that treated any non-zero code other than 1 as a usage
    error needs to read 3 as a refusal.
  * Mail classes: a credential has a class, `personal` (the default),
    `marketing` or `transactional`, set with `app-password create --class`,
    `api-key create --class` and `account set NAME --default-class`. The class
    is a floor: the `class` field of an API request or a JMAP submission may
    ask for a stricter one, never a looser one, and an `Epistle-Class` header
    changes nothing and is removed. `marketing` needs a one-click unsubscribe
    and never goes to a suppressed address. `transactional` is not copied to
    Sent, and is refused (SMTP `451 4.3.0`, API `503`, JMAP
    `serverUnavailable`) when its evidence cannot be written; the server takes
    none until it has verified the log once after a start.
  * Evidence of sending: acceptances, outcomes (delivered, bounced, expired,
    suppressed, abandoned, canceled) and key changes go into a hash-chained,
    append-only log with Merkle inclusion proofs, a signed checkpoint for each
    closed day and an RFC 3161 timestamp on it (Sectigo's public service by
    default); without a policy, `transactional` keeps metadata for 365 days.
    `epistle evidence
    verify|export|check|key|policy|timestamp|clock|acknowledge|abandon`.
    `evidence check` verifies a bundle offline and fails without `--key`: the
    reader brings the signing key (a fingerprint or a PEM file; an earlier key
    as `KEY@YYYY-MM-DD`), because a bundle cannot vouch for the key that signed
    it. `--require-delivered` makes it exit 1 unless every recipient was
    delivered. It is not certified email and does not prove that anyone read a
    message. See docs/evidence.md.
  * Antispam: `[attachments]` judges each attachment by its bytes, not its
    name: the real type against the name and the declared type, double and
    always-dangerous extensions (exe, scr, js, vbs, bat, lnk, iso and others,
    which `allow_extensions` cannot allow), zip entry names read without
    extracting, Office macros, encrypted and nested archives, SVG, and
    executables and zips hidden in images. What cannot be read to the end is
    never clean. `mode = off|flag|quarantine|reject` (`flag` adds a codes-only
    `X-Epistle-Attachment-Warning` header, which is stripped from inbound mail;
    `reject` is a 550 during DATA), plus `macros` and `encrypted_archives`.
    `epistle attachments policy show|set|unset` edits it and `epistle
    attachments check FILE.eml` judges a local message; with `--strict` it
    exits 3 on a quarantine or reject. See docs/attachments.md.
  * Antispam: `clamd_on_error = accept|defer`. With `defer`, a message clamd
    cannot scan gets `451 4.7.1` and never reaches a mailbox unscanned, and one
    clamd can never take (over `clamd_max_bytes`) is refused for good: `552
    5.3.4` on SMTP, `413 scan_too_large` on the API, `forbiddenToSend` on JMAP.
    `defer` requires `clamd_max_bytes` of at least `max_message_size` + 64 KiB,
    and the configuration is refused otherwise.
  * Queue: IP warm-up per receiver group (gmail, outlook, yahoo, icloud and
    other, read from the MX that answers). Each group has a daily cap that
    starts at 50 and is paced through the UTC day; recipients over it wait in
    the spool, which is not an attempt and does not count toward expiry. Each
    day is judged from the delivery log by reply codes: a good day raises the
    cap by half when at least half of it was used, a very bad one (over 10%
    deferrals, or a 5.7.x reply) lowers it, never under 50. `epistle warmup
    status|plan|start|enable|disable|pause|resume|set|reset`, all with
    `--json`; `status`, `queue list` and `queue destinations` show it. See
    docs/warmup.md.
  * Queue: delivery is concurrent and bounded. Every step of an attempt has a
    timeout and a whole attempt ends after 1 hour, but never once the final dot
    is sent. There is a global cap (8 by default), a cap per destination domain
    and per exchanger, and a sender takes at most 3/4 of the slots. A 421 or
    4.7.x reply puts the destination on hold for one backoff step, kept across
    restarts. `epistle limits set|unset|show
    queue-concurrency|queue-per-domain-concurrency|queue-backoff`, `epistle
    queue destinations` and `epistle queue release-destination
    DOMAIN|HOST|--all`. A broken spool entry no longer stops the scan.
  * Stack: `epistle stack resources [set SERVICE --memory SIZE --pids N]
    [--json]` lists and changes the container limits in compose.yaml (`mail` is
    refused less than five messages of `max_message_size` plus 256 MiB), and
    `epistle status` has a `resources` area. On a new install `epistle init`
    sizes the containers from the host memory (ClamAV is left out under 4 GiB);
    an existing install is not changed, and `status` suggests the commands.
  * DKIM: `[[dkim.domain]]` (`name`, `selector`, `key_file`, optional RSA
    selector and key) gives a domain its own signing key; `[dkim]` stays the
    default for the others. A domain that is not hosted, or listed twice, or
    with a missing or invalid key stops the start. `dns-records`, `verify-dns`,
    `status` and `dkim show` show each domain's own selector. Overridden
    domains are not rotated, and `dkim rotate --domain` refuses them. `epistle
    migrate mox` imports each domain's key as such an override.
  * DKIM and TLS: the automatic rotation now renews the RSA key too (2048 bits,
    selector `rsa<day>`) in the same journal entry as the ed25519 key: both
    switch together or neither does. With your own certificate (no ACME) the
    mail listeners check the files every 60 s and swap in a new pair only when
    the whole chain parses and the key matches; a bad pair is logged once and
    the old one stays. The TLSA record is republished through the DNS provider,
    or a warning gives the value to publish by hand. `epistle status` warns
    when such a certificate expires within 14 days and nothing renews it.
  * JMAP: `VacationResponse` (RFC 8621 section 8). JMAP and Sieve share one
    auto-reply path: an enabled JMAP vacation wins, otherwise a Sieve
    `vacation` answers. A reply goes out only if the sender is not a robot or a
    list (`List-*`, `Precedence: bulk|list|junk`, `Auto-Submitted`,
    `MAILER-DAEMON`), an address of the account is in To or Cc, the server's
    own SPF or DKIM check passed for the envelope sender's domain, and none
    went to that sender in the period (1 to 365 days). It goes to the envelope
    sender, carries `Auto-Submitted: auto-replied` and is DKIM signed.
  * IMAP: `THREAD=REFERENCES` (RFC 5256), the threading most clients use,
    computed without recursion so a hostile mailbox cannot overflow the stack,
    and `APPENDLIMIT=26214400` (RFC 7889): an APPEND over it gets `NO [TOOBIG]`
    and the connection stays open, where it used to close with BYE.
  * DNS: `epistle status` and `verify-dns` count the records with each tag at a
    name and fail when there is more than one SPF, DMARC, MTA-STS or TLS-RPT
    record, or the same DKIM selector twice (two SPF records make every
    receiver return `permerror`). Publishing now edits the first record of the
    purpose and deletes the other TXT records with that tag at that name, for
    every provider; TXT records of another purpose are left alone. `epistle
    status` also fails when a mail name is proxied by Cloudflare (orange
    cloud), and publishing sends `proxied: false` for them. A null MX no longer
    goes out with an empty host on GoDaddy, Spaceship, Porkbun and Namecheap,
    and Google Cloud DNS reads every page of a zone.
  * CLI: `epistle alias`, `domain`, `relay` and `alert` naming an unknown
    target exit 2, as other unknown names do, where they exited 1. `account
    add`, `remove`, `password`, `enable` and `disable` wait up to 5 s until the
    running server has loaded the change, so a script that adds an account and
    logs in at once no longer trips the brute-force ban; on timeout they note
    it and exit 0. `account show` shows forwards, catch-all and two-factor for
    accounts defined in mail.toml too, `account add` validates the names before
    asking for the password, and creating or removing an app password keeps
    what another process wrote meanwhile.
  * CLI: `epistle domain disable|enable` switches a hosted domain off and on
    without removing it. The new `disabled_domains` setting lists the
    switched-off domains; mail for one is rejected like mail for an unknown
    user and nobody may send as an address in it. Accounts, DNS records and
    DKIM keys stay. `domain list` and `domain show` report `enabled`. A
    disabled domain gets no autoconfig/autodiscover document, `GET
    /api/v1/domains` names it under `disabled`, `masked add` refuses it and
    `account add`/`alias add` warn about it. `domain disable` says how many
    queued messages still have a sender in the domain (they are delivered), and
    the docs state that a login with an address in the disabled domain fails.
  * CLI: `epistle domain set` writes the storage quota and the submission
    rate of one domain (the overrides `limits set --domain` already wrote),
    and `domain show` prints them.
  * CLI: `epistle alert show|enable|disable`. A rule switched off with the new
    `enabled = false` key stays in the file but takes no samples and never
    fires; `alert list` marks it and its JSON row has `enabled`.
  * CLI: `epistle webhook enable|disable`. `enabled = false` in `[webhook]`
    keeps the URL and the secret but posts no event, alerts included; `webhook
    test` still posts. `webhook show` prints `enabled`. `webhook disable` lists
    the events and alerts that go quiet, needs `--yes` while an alert posts to
    the webhook, and `config-check` warns about such an alert.
  * CLI: `epistle relay enable|disable`. A relay rule with `enabled = false`
    stays in the file but is never selected, so its mail goes by the next
    matching rule or direct to the MX. Only a relay rule can be switched off;
    the loader refuses `enabled = false` on a `fail` or `direct` rule.
    `relay disable` says where the mail will go instead (the next matching
    rule, or directly to the MX from this server's own address) and needs
    `--yes` off a terminal.
  * Fix: `epistle queue remove` panicked with "there is no reactor running"
    when a webhook was configured (the command line had no runtime to post the
    `canceled` event on); it now posts it before exiting.
  * CLI: `epistle suppression show|export|import`, and `suppression add
    --reason`. `export` writes the list as CSV, `import` loads such a file or
    a plain list of addresses (16 MiB and 100000 entries at most, checked whole
    before anything is written). `suppression add` no longer says it
    suppressed an address whose marker it could not write, and `suppression
    list` escapes control characters in what it prints.
  * CLI: `epistle app-password rotate|rename`. `rotate` replaces the secret
    and prints the new one once, keeping the label, expiry, address range and
    `save-sent` setting; the old secret stops working at once. `rename` keeps
    the secret. App password labels are now 1 to 64 characters without
    control characters when created or renamed, and `app-password list`
    escapes control characters.
  * CLI: `epistle delivery show ID` prints every record of one message and
    `delivery export` writes the filtered records as CSV (or JSON), at most
    100000. `delivery list` takes `--until`, and `--status` as another name
    of `--outcome`. The delivery log is now read a line at a time (a line over
    1 MiB is skipped), days outside `--since`/`--until` are not opened, and
    `delivery list --limit` stops at 100000 like `export`.
  * CLI: `epistle db status` prints the PostgreSQL version, size, tables and
    connections from one read-only query in the stack's database container,
    and gives up after 15 seconds, stopping what the query started with it.
  * DNS providers: a listing keeps every record under its own type. NS, HTTPS,
    SVCB and CAA were read as TXT by some providers and dropped by others; a
    type epistle has no use for (NAPTR, the SOA, Namecheap's URL redirects,
    Bunny's redirect and script records) is kept under its type name. A type
    name that is not letters, digits and hyphens (at most 32) shows as UNKNOWN.
    OVH and Porkbun no longer cut the closing quote off a CAA or HTTPS value.
  * DNS providers: every call has a 10 second connect and a 30 second total
    timeout, an operation (a listing with all its pages, a publish with its
    reads) a 120 second deadline, redirects are not followed, and a response
    body past 16 MiB (256 KiB for Namecheap) is refused as it arrives.
    Before, twelve providers waited for ever and read any body whole; RFC 2136
    now has its own connect and exchange timeouts and the same deadline. The
    bodies of one operation share a 64 MiB budget. A listing past 1000 pages
    or 100000 records is an error for every provider; one whose pages do not
    add up to the total it gives is an error (Spaceship, Cloudflare, DNSimple,
    DigitalOcean); a full page that does not say whether more follow is an
    error (Cloudflare, DNSimple, Spaceship); and deSEC lists zones of more
    than 500 rrsets page by page. A listing keeps the preference of an MX and
    the flags and tag of a CAA, and Spaceship sends and reads the MX field as
    `preference`; those field layouts come from the providers' API references
    and are not checked against the real services.
  * DNS: `epistle dns lockdown [DOMAIN] [--dry-run] [--yes] [--json] [--undo]
    [--dmarc reject]` stops anyone from sending mail as the domain from names
    that do not send mail. It reads the zone through the DNS provider and plans
    a null SPF (`v=spf1 -all`) and a null MX (`0 .`) on every name with an
    address that does not send mail (skipping the names epistle serves and
    those with their own MX, SPF or CNAME), a wildcard null SPF when the zone
    has no wildcard, and DMARC one step per run (none, quarantine, reject;
    `--dmarc reject` jumps to reject). Nothing is written without confirmation.
    A journal lets `--undo` remove only the records that still match what was
    written; a record the operator changed since is left alone.
  * `epistle dns lockdown` leaves delegations alone: a name below the zone
    apex with NS records, and every name under it, gets no record and is
    listed once as delegated to other nameservers. A domain that is itself
    delegated gets no DMARC rewrite and no wildcard. The plan is built in one
    pass over the zone, a name that cannot be a DNS name is reported cut down,
    and control characters from the zone are escaped in the output. A name
    that is not letters, digits, hyphens and underscores is listed as left
    alone and never written to, a wildcard hidden by a delegation does not
    block the domain's wildcard, and `--undo` leaves a record in a name
    delegated since. See docs/dns.md.

 -- Glyndor <packages@glyndor.net>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 19:27:09 +0000

epistle (0.12.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Migration: `epistle migrate imap` copies a remote account from any IMAP
    server (resumable, keeps folders, flags and dates), and `epistle migrate
    mox` / `epistle migrate stalwart` import domains, accounts, aliases and
    DKIM keys with their selectors, so DNS does not change. See
    docs/migration.md.
  * Subfolders: mailbox names may contain `/` (Work/Project) in IMAP, JMAP
    (parentId), Sieve fileinto and Maildir export/import. CREATE makes the
    missing parents, RENAME moves children, DELETE of a parent with children
    answers HASCHILDREN. No data migration.
  * Sent copies: mail an account sends through SMTP submission, the API or
    JMAP is saved in its Sent folder by default, without duplicating the copy
    a client appends itself. `epistle account save-sent`, `app-password
    create --save-sent` and `"save_sent": false` on the API turn it off.
  * Delivery log: one metadata-only line per recipient outcome under
    data_dir/deliverylog, kept 30 days ([storage] delivery_log_days, 0 turns
    it off), read with `epistle delivery list` or GET /api/v1/deliveries.
    New webhooks delivered, delayed, suppressed and canceled, and an `extra`
    correlation map on /api/v1/send.
  * IMAP, checked with Dovecot's imaptest in CI (69 of 73 scripts pass; the
    rest test \Recent, which IMAP4rev2 removed): other sessions' expunges
    and flag changes are announced in order, STORE no longer undoes another
    session's change, SEARCH supports BODY, SENT*, every HEADER field and
    parsed addresses, CONDSTORE is remembered, SORT and THREAD follow RFC
    5256, APPEND keeps its date and zone, ENVELOPE, BINARY and message/rfc822
    parts are complete, UIDVALIDITY is never reused, NOTIFY SET STATUS
    works, LIST understands wildcards and the reference, and the tail of a
    rejected literal is no longer read as a command.
  * Security: IMAP lines are bounded at 32 KiB with linear scanning, search
    nesting at 64 levels, the base subject is computed in linear time, and
    the migration client bounds memory by the bytes it receives.
  * CLI: run as root, `epistle` hands the files an operator names
    (password files, a Mox config, an import or export Maildir) to the
    service account through a private staging copy, and moves outputs
    back owned by root; `epistle delivery list` is the delivery log
    reader.


