epistle (0.10.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Operate the server with commands instead of editing files: status and
    check, service, limits, account, alias, domain, masked, queue, ban,
    suppression, antispam, relay, dns provider, storage blobs, webhook,
    alert, dkim, tls and api-key. Changes to mail.toml keep its comments,
    are validated before they are written, accept --dry-run and restart
    mail only when needed. See the common tasks table in docs/cli.md.
  * epistle probe checks every listener and pins TLS listeners to the
    configured certificate; the generated compose file uses it as the
    mail container's healthcheck.
  * DKIM rotation keeps every signing domain valid: the new selector is
    published for each domain in the managed zone before signing switches,
    the RSA signature survives a rotation, and a restart signs with the
    rotated key. Rotation is off, with a warning, when a signing domain
    is outside the zone.
  * A renewed certificate reaches IMAP, POP3 and ManageSieve too, renewal
    is decided from the certificate's notAfter, and a restart presents the
    ACME certificate.
  * API keys created or revoked with the CLI take effect without a
    restart.
  * Account and mailbox names are validated where they become paths.
  * A plain-http webhook is accepted only for a real loopback host.
  * JMAP enforces maxCallsInRequest and the advertised maxSizeRequest.
  * Commands that need the database (ban, account-remove, check db) work
    from the host when the database only listens inside the containers,
    and a listener change recreates mail so its published ports follow.
  * Logs name an account by a keyed pseudonym instead of its name;
    epistle account show prints it and epistle account find-log-id maps
    it back.
  * epistle oauth-keygen writes the key pair to the file given with
    --out (mode 0600) and no longer prints the private key; scripts that
    read it from standard output must pass --out.
  * Credentials sent to DNS providers and OIDC endpoints only travel over
    https, enforced by type; DigitalOcean pagination no longer follows a
    link to another origin, and Google Cloud DNS request paths are right.
  * Every path under the data directory is checked to stay inside it.
  * IMAP LOGIN and AUTHENTICATE credentials never travel through the
    general command type.

